# 12. RECORD ARCHITECTURE AND AUDIT CONTROLS

Each assessment should generate a structured record.

### 12.1 Minimum record fields

|Field|Purpose|
|---|---|
|Record ID|Unique identifier|
|Source reference|Origin of the information|
|Created timestamp|Record creation time|
|Observed timestamp|Time of reported event|
|Ingested timestamp|Time entered into the system|
|Responsible role|Assigned record owner|
|Authority reference|Governing authority|
|Evidence reference|Supporting documentation|
|Confidence level|Evidence authentication status|
|Assessment score|Component measurement|
|Reviewer|Person conducting assessment|
|Review status|Pending, completed or disputed|
|Corrective action|Required response|
|Closure reference|Documented resolution|

### 12.2 Audit controls

- Preserve original submissions.
- Maintain version histories.
- Restrict unauthorized modifications.
- Separate evidence collection from final assessment.
- Document reviewer conflicts of interest.
- Preserve dissenting opinions.
- Require independent validation for material findings.
- Maintain correction and closure histories.

The system should never silently overwrite an earlier record or erase a disagreement simply because a later institutional conclusion has been issued.

